{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for task/whoami-truthful-orientation at exact head 8b944700483d3f7a2d467c82ed3f6b6c104a784a.\n\nI found no behavioural defect. Every guard I exercised does what the ready-for-review report says it does, and the live behaviour at real signed depth is right. All the findings have the same shape: a statement that carries the truthfulness claim, which no test in the repository can make fail. The conditions asked for mutation-sensitive coverage and the report offers a mutation sweep as its evidence, so I am returning these rather than approving with notes. Each repair is small and local.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED, AND HOW\n\nHead and hygiene. The worktree /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/whoami-truthful-orientation was at 8b944700483d3f7a2d467c82ed3f6b6c104a784a and clean before and after the whole pass; I never wrote to it. Current main 0a66e8595a4ca0b3378f7792d0eb5f94d3609db2 is an ancestor. All three commits carry Rests-On: 8b94470 and bb893ea on ee797a9f, 963ea87 on 906ea8cf. git diff --check 0a66e859..HEAD is clean.\n\nGates at this head. make vet, go build ./..., make test and go test -race ./... all pass. UI tests are 34/34. The diff touches no UI file; I still rebuilt the UI twice in a disposable worktree, and both builds produced spike/internal/service/uidist byte-identical to each other and to the committed bytes.\n\nLive behaviour, not only httptest. I built the reviewed gitseq-mcp and drove it over stdio.\n- Against the resident running on 127.0.0.1:7777, which predates the endpoint and answers 404, whoami returned degraded true, source verified_signed_checkpoint_tail, the exact frontier at depth 1177, no key_file, in 0.81 s wall for the whole process. Falling back to an older resident works.\n- Against a reviewed resident I started on an isolated copy-on-write copy of the repository at depth 1178, whoami returned source resident_statusview_current, degraded false, repeatably in about 0.05 s wall for the whole process. The durable payload carried name, fingerprint, kind, membership_event and roles only: no role_sources, no custody path. The first request to that freshly started resident took 0.49 s, so a cold resident does serve the fast path within the client's budget.\nI pointed no writer at the live workroom.\n\nMutation sweep. In a separate detached worktree at the same head I removed each guard in turn and ran the test that should notice, restoring the file between runs. Eighteen mutations were caught, so these are real evidence: current-head equality, local-genesis equality, fingerprint equality, the role cap, roles_skipped non-negativity, the participant membership check, DisallowUnknownFields, the trailing-JSON check, redirect refusal, loopback host validation, the retry bound 2 to 1, publicActor custody trimming, both provenance classifications in app.go (checkpoint inversion and cold-audit mislabel), CapRoles inside BuildOrientation, the two size guards removed as a pair, the two JSON-strictness guards removed as a pair, and forcing the resident answer to be discarded. That last one matters: TestWhoamiUsesBoundedEffectiveResidentOrientationWithoutLocalReplay deletes an event blob, so the test genuinely proves the fast path performs no local fold.\n\nFINDINGS\n\nF1, MUST FIX. The projection-version guard cannot fail. spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:499, the term `orientation.ProjectionVersion != service.OrientationProjectionVersion`, can be replaced with `false` and `go test ./cmd/gitseq-mcp ./internal/service ./internal/statusview` stays green. No test serves an orientation with a wrong or missing projection_version. This is the one guard that answers version skew, where a resident agreeing on genesis and head has different actor-projection semantics, and skew is the realistic trigger rather than malice: gs serve is long-lived while gitseq-mcp is spawned per session. Two smaller terms of the same predicate are unpinned in the same way and worth closing in the same test: `orientation.Frontier.Depth \u003c 0` at main.go:501, and the three emptiness checks `You.Name == \"\" || You.Kind == \"\" || You.MembershipEvent == \"\"` at main.go:502. Each survives the full ./cmd/gitseq-mcp suite.\n\nF2, MUST FIX. One of the three provenance labels cannot fail. spike/internal/app/app.go:741, inside acceptSnapshot, sets `w.snapshotSource = SnapshotSourceIncrementalTail`. Change it to SnapshotSourceColdFullAudit and ./internal/app, ./cmd/gitseq-mcp, ./cmd/gs and ./internal/service all stay green. That line is reached whenever a process submits an act and then folds; a later whoami with the resident unavailable would report a cold full audit it never performed. The Load-path classification a few lines below is properly pinned by TestWhoamiDisclosesCheckpointAndFullAuditFallbacks, and TestSnapshotCachesTheVerifiedHead pins the incremental label on the Load path, but nothing reaches the acceptSnapshot assignment. \"Never label checkpoint recovery as full audit\" is a stated condition; the converse mislabel is currently free.\n\nF3, SHOULD FIX. The two-second and 64 KiB numbers are not pinned, because the tests read the constants under test. main_test.go:634 asserts `elapsed \u003e orientationTimeout+time.Second`, so raising orientationTimeout from 2 s to 20 s leaves the stall test green; I ran it and it passed after 21 s. main_test.go:547 builds the oversize payload with `strings.Repeat(\"x\", orientationResponseLimit)`, so the payload grows with the bound. What is pinned in both cases is the mechanism, not the value: substituting a different literal duration inside context.WithTimeout does fail the stall test, and removing both size guards does fail the oversize subtest. The conditions name \"two-second, 64-KiB\" specifically, so at least one assertion in each pair should use a literal.\n\nOne accuracy point for the next report rather than a finding. The ready-for-review text says guard-removal made the byte-limit test fail. Removing the length check at main.go:592 alone does not fail the oversize subtest: the io.LimitReader truncation then makes the payload malformed and it is rejected for a different reason. Removing both size guards does fail it, so the bound is pinned as a pair. The claim is true of the behaviour and not of the single line, and a later reader will rely on the distinction.\n\nOBSERVATIONS I DELIBERATELY DID NOT RAISE AS FINDINGS\n\nThe loopback trust boundary. Any process that binds the configured loopback port can make whoami state a falsehood with degraded false. I reproduced it: a stub returning the real genesis, the real current head, the real fingerprint and the real projection version, but name hugh, kind human, an all-zero membership event and roles participant, operator, ratifier, was accepted and reported as source resident_statusview_current. I am not raising this against this head. The MCP client already returns the resident's answer for status and wait with no verification at all, so whoami on this path is strictly more careful than the tools around it, and the fold, not whoami, decides authority, so nothing is actually conferred. It is a boundary question for a separate request if the project wants whoami to be the one locally grounded answer, and my prior F1 on the superseded Task 2e head is only partly retired by this design: the version guard and the loopback validation I asked for are here, the local grounding of meaning-bearing fields is not.\n\nFrontier contention pushes you to the slowest path. Two head moves inside the retry window drop the client to a local fold, which is the slowest option precisely when the workroom is busiest. Correct and honest; worth knowing the direction of failure.\n\nA cold resident's first fold is unbounded. If it exceeds two seconds the client pays a local cold audit on top of the wait. The checkpoint keeps this at about half a second in practice, measured above.\n\ngitseq-mcp now refuses a non-loopback --server at startup, which affects every tool and not only whoami. This matches the loopback-only listen that gs serve already enforces and every documented invocation, so it is not a regression, but docs/reference.md does not mention it. https to a loopback address is refused along with everything else that is not plain http; deliberate, noted so it does not surprise anyone later.\n\nSmall things. spike/internal/statusview/actor.go:40 reads \"You reuses the capped actor view\"; it should read \"It reuses\". The whoami tool description at main.go:373 still says \"Show the configured durable actor and ephemeral session\" and does not mention frontier, source or degraded. The fingerprint is interpolated into the request path unescaped, which is safe because fingerprints are hex. gofmt reports spike/internal/nexus/session_privacy_test.go, equally unformatted on main and untouched here.\n\nI removed the scratch worktree and the disposable copies. The reviewed worktree is unchanged and still at 8b944700483d3f7a2d467c82ed3f6b6c104a784a.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:165610ed5152ce84d641a3fc7d21c358e65892a4","head":"8b944700483d3f7a2d467c82ed3f6b6c104a784a","verdict":"changes-requested"}}